# Contributing

Thanks for helping with Open Responses. This file focuses on how to make proposals.

## Proposals

We keep changes open, neutral, and interoperable, as described in the [technical charter](CHARTER.md).

## Maintainers

The [Maintainers](MAINTAINERS.md) are the initial voting members of the Technical Steering Committee (TSC).
Maintainer selection and removal follow the technical charter.

### When to propose

Create a proposal for any change that affects the specification, compatibility, or conformance.

### How to propose

1. Open an issue titled "Proposal: <short name>" with:
   - Problem statement and motivation
   - Proposed change (spec text or API shape)
   - Compatibility impact (breaking, additive, or clarifying)
   - Reference implementation or migration plan (if applicable)
   - Test or conformance plan
2. After initial feedback, submit a PR that includes the spec change and related docs/tests.

The contract is authored in TypeSpec under `schema/`. Add Open Responses metadata,
including `x-openresponses-added-in`, to those definitions and run `bun run spec`.
Use the `AddedIn` component for matching prose. `bun run spec:check` verifies that
the generated schema matches the published file. See `schema/README.md` for the
source layout and contract comparison tools.

When the release is ready, update the version in `schema/routes.tsp` and run
`bun run spec:release` to write `public/openapi/YYYY-MM-DD/openapi.json`.
Dated artifacts are immutable: release generation stops if that release date
already has a different snapshot.

A release also gets an immutable prose snapshot, generated Specification and Reference routes,
and an entry in `CHANGELOG.md`.

### How proposals are decided

We seek consensus. If consensus cannot be reached, decisions follow the TSC voting
and escalation procedures in the technical charter.

### DCO

All code contributions must include a Developer Certificate of Origin (DCO) sign-off.
